# Nielsen & Chuang, Problem 4.3 (Alternate universality construction) — Part 1, Hermitian generator

Alternate universality: expand H=i ln U in Pauli products; approximate U via Trotter.

## What this file formalises

This file contains the **existence half of Part (1)**: every unitary evolution is generated by a
Hamiltonian. In this library a closed-system dynamics is an `Evolution` (a unitary) and a
Hamiltonian is an `Observable` (a self-adjoint operator); `Observable.propagator H 1 0 1` is the
unit-time, `ℏ = 1` propagator `exp(-i H)` (Nielsen–Chuang eq. 2.91). The forward direction
"`exp(-iH)` is a unitary evolution" is already the content of `Observable.propagator` (N&C
Exercise 2.55). The theorem here is the **converse**:

* `Evolution.exists_hamiltonian_generator` — for any `Evolution U` on a (finite-dimensional)
  quantum system `S`, there is an `Observable H` with `U = H.propagator 1 0 1`, i.e. `U = exp(-iH)`
  for a Hermitian `H`. This is exactly "`H = i ln U` is Hermitian" (Part 1): `U` has a Hermitian
  generator. In finite dimensions this is the converse of Stone's theorem — every unitary is the
  exponential of `i` times a self-adjoint operator.

It also contains the circuit-approximation statements of Part (6), listed below.

## Scope

* **The Hermitian generator, not a specific branch.** Part (1) also asserts the eigenvalues of `H`
  lie in `[0, 2π]`. That is a statement about a *particular* choice of branch for `ln`; it refines
  the existence proved here. `exists_hamiltonian_generator` is a complete, faithful theorem on its
  own — the Hermitian generator exists — not a weakened version of Part (1).
* **Part (1b), the eigenvalue range.** The refinement "`H` can be chosen with spectrum in
  `[0, 2π]`" is
  `AxQM.Evolution.exists_hamiltonian_generator_spectrum_Icc`
  (`AxQM/Postulates/API/HamiltonianGenerator.lean`): for any `Evolution U` there is an
  `Observable H` with `U = H.propagator 1 0 1` **and** `spectrum ℂ H.op ⊆ ℝ ∩ [0, 2π]` (every
  eigenvalue a real number in `[0, 2π]`). It is proved from the general lemma
  `ContinuousLinearMap.exists_isSelfAdjoint_spectrum_Icc_exp_neg_smul_I_eq_of_mem_unitary`
  (`ToMathlib/Analysis/CStarAlgebra/Unitary/ExpEndomorphism.lean`), built from the `[0, 2π)`-branch
  of the logarithm via the continuous functional calculus.
* **`n` qubits vs. a general system.** N&C fix a unitary on `n` qubits (dimension `2ⁿ`); the theorem
  is stated for an arbitrary finite-dimensional system `S`, which is strictly more general and
  specialises to the `n`-qubit register. Part (1) makes no use of the tensor factorisation — that
  enters only in Part (2)'s Pauli expansion.
* **The mathematics.** The operator-level fact "every unitary on a finite-dimensional Hilbert space
  is `exp(i • A)` for self-adjoint `A`" is the coordinate-free form of N&C Exercise 2.56; it is
  `ContinuousLinearMap.exists_isSelfAdjoint_exp_smul_I_eq_of_mem_unitary`
  (`ToMathlib/Analysis/CStarAlgebra/Unitary/ExpEndomorphism.lean`). Here `H.op = -A`, so that
  `H.propagator 1 0 1 = exp(-i H) = exp(i • A) = U`.
* **Part (2), the Pauli expansion.** This is
  `AxQM.Observable.exists_real_pauliString_expansion`
  (`AxQM/Postulates/API/PauliExpansion.lean`): for a Hamiltonian `H : Observable (qudit
  (2ⁿ))` on the `n`-qubit register, its computational-basis matrix — reindexed to bit strings `Fin
  n → Fin 2` — is `Σ_g (h_g : ℂ) • pauliString g` with real `h_g`. It rests on the matrix-level
  basis result `AxQM.Concrete.exists_real_pauliString_expansion`
  (`AxQM/Concrete/PauliString.lean`). Combined with `Evolution.exists_hamiltonian_generator`
  above it gives part (2) for the generator `H = i ln U`.
* **Parts (4) and (5): the Banach-algebra lemmas.** Part (4)'s first-order Trotter / Lie
  product-formula error and part (5)'s `k`-fold accumulation are, at the operator level, the
  lemmas
  `NormedSpace.exists_norm_prod_exp_smul_sub_exp_smul_sum_le`
  (`‖(∏ᵢ exp(t•gᵢ)) - exp(t • ∑ᵢ gᵢ)‖ ≤ C·t²`) and
  `NormedSpace.exists_norm_prod_exp_inv_smul_pow_sub_exp_sum_le`
  (`‖(∏ᵢ exp(k⁻¹•gᵢ))ᵏ - exp(∑ᵢ gᵢ)‖ ≤ C·k⁻¹`), both in
  `ToMathlib/Analysis/Normed/Algebra/LieTrotter.lean`. Instantiating `gᵢ = -i hᵢ Pᵢ` (the part (2)
  Pauli expansion of `H = i ln U`) and `t = Δ = 1/k` yields exactly (4)
  `exp(-iHΔ) = ∏ᵢ exp(-i hᵢ Pᵢ Δ) + O(Δ²)` and (5) `U = [∏ᵢ exp(-i hᵢ Pᵢ Δ)]ᵏ + O(Δ)`; the `4ⁿ`
  factor of N&C is absorbed into the constant `C`.
* **Parts (4) and (5) for observables.** The two bounds above are transferred to observables as
  `AxQM.Observable.exists_norm_prod_propagator_op_sub_propagator_op_le` (part (4)) and
  `AxQM.Observable.exists_norm_prod_propagator_op_pow_sub_propagator_op_le` (part (5))
  (`AxQM/Postulates/API/TrotterProduct.lean`): for a Hamiltonian decomposition
  `H = Σᵢ Hᵢ` into `Observable`s, `‖(∏ᵢ (Hᵢ.propagator 1 0 Δ).op) - (H.propagator 1 0 Δ).op‖ ≤ C·Δ²`
  and `‖(∏ᵢ (Hᵢ.propagator 1 0 (1/k)).op)ᵏ - (H.propagator 1 0 1).op‖ ≤ C/k`, with
  `(H.propagator 1 0 1).op = exp(-iH) = U`. The operator algebra `S.space →L[ℂ] S.space` is itself
  the Banach algebra of the lemmas above, so no matrix transport is needed. Specialising the `Hᵢ`
  to the Pauli-string Hamiltonians `hᵢ Pᵢ` of part (2) recovers N&C's literal `∏_g exp(-i h_g g Δ)`
  form, as follows.
* **The literal Pauli-string Trotter form and the main theorem.** The specialisation of the general
  (4)/(5) bounds to the actual Pauli-string decomposition is in
  `AxQM/Postulates/API/PauliTrotter.lean`. There
  `AxQM.pauliStringHamiltonian g c` is the summand `c · g` (a Pauli string scaled by a real
  `c`, built as an `Observable` on `qudit (2ⁿ)` via `Matrix.toEuclideanCLM`, using the reverse
  operator↔matrix bridge `AxQM.Observable.toEuclideanCLM_stdMatrix`);
  `AxQM.Observable.op_eq_sum_pauliStringHamiltonian` is part (2) in operator form
  (`H.op = Σ_g (hᵢ Pᵢ).op`, transported from the matrix expansion); and
  `AxQM.Observable.exists_norm_prod_pauliStringHamiltonian_propagator_sub_le` (4) /
  `..._pow_sub_le` (5) are the literal bounds `‖∏_g exp(-i h_g g Δ) - exp(-iHΔ)‖ ≤ C·Δ²` and
  `‖[∏_g exp(-i h_g g /k)]ᵏ - exp(-iH)‖ ≤ C/k` over the `4ⁿ` Pauli-string propagators. The theorem
  `AxQM.Evolution.exists_pauliString_trotter_approximation` assembles parts (1), (2), (5):
  *every* `n`-qubit unitary `U` satisfies `‖[∏_g exp(-i h_g g /k)]ᵏ - U‖ ≤ C/k`.
* **Parts (3) and (6): gate-count constructions.** Part (3) ("implement `exp(-i h_g g Δ)` using
  `O(n)` one- and two-qubit gates") and part (6) ("approximate `U` to within `ε` using
  `O(n 16ⁿ/ε)` operations") are *implement-this-unitary-with-`N`-gates* claims of exactly the form
  the library's elementary-gate cost model `AxQM.CircuitBudget` (`Core/CircuitBudget.lean`)
  expresses. Problem 4.1 formalises its `2T + n` gate count through this same model
  (`diagPhase_computablePhaseAngle_circuitBudget`), and the asymptotic construction of Problem 4.2
  ("depth `O(log n)` for `Cⁿ(X)`") is also a gate-count statement. The construction is the standard
  cascade: single-qubit Cliffords diagonalising each Pauli of `g` to `Z`, a `CNOT` cascade
  collapsing `Z^{⊗supp}` to one target qubit, a single `Z`-rotation `exp(-i h_g Δ Z)`, then the
  uncomputation — `O(n)` elementary gates, so `CircuitBudget … 0 (O(n))` via
  `CircuitBudget.listProd`; part (6) then multiplies the `4ⁿ` Pauli summands by the
  `k = O(4ⁿ/ε)` Trotter repetitions of part (5). The pieces are listed in the following items.
* **Part (3), the closed form of a Trotter summand.** The concrete operator each part-(3) circuit
  must implement is
  `AxQM.pauliStringHamiltonian_propagator_op`
  (`AxQM/Postulates/API/InvolutiveObservable.lean`): the summand `exp(-i c t g)` of a
  Pauli-string Hamiltonian `c · g` is the explicit rotation `cos(c t) · I - i sin(c t) · g` (with
  `g = (pauliStringHamiltonian g 1).op`, an involution `g² = I`), a direct specialisation of the
  general `H.op = c • G`, `G² = I` propagator-rotation law
  `AxQM.Observable.propagator_op_of_op_eq_smul_involution`
  (`AxQM/Postulates/API/InvolutionPropagatorConjugation.lean`). It exhibits each
  `exp(-i h_g g Δ)` as a genuine rotation whose axis is the Pauli string — the target the `O(n)`
  cascade diagonalises to a single-qubit `Z`-rotation.
* **Part (3), basis-change diagonalisation of an arbitrary Pauli string.** The `O(n)` circuit's
  opening move is the layer of single-qubit gates conjugating each Pauli factor of `g` to `Z`
  (Hadamard for `X`, `S·H` for `Y`, identity for `Z`/`I`), leaving a Hamiltonian that is `Z` on the
  string's support and `I` elsewhere. At the matrix level this is
  `AxQM.Concrete.pauliStringStd_exp_smul_conj_kronFamilyStd_pauliDiagGate`
  (`AxQM/Concrete/PauliStringDiagonalize.lean`): for any Pauli string `g : Fin n → Fin 4`
  and scalar `s`, `exp(s · g) = B · exp(s · d(g)) · B†` on the flat register `Fin (2ⁿ)`, where
  `B = ⨂ₖ B_{gₖ}` is the tensor of per-qubit basis-change gates (`pauliDiagGate`) and `d(g)` is the
  diagonalised string (`pauliToZ`, every non-identity factor sent to `Z`). It generalises the
  `X ⊗ Y ⊗ Z` reduction of Exercise 4.51 (`Concrete/PauliStringBasisChange.lean`) to a general
  string.
* **Part (3), the basis-change layer `B` as a product of single-wire gates.** To *count* the
  basis-change layer `B = ⨂ₖ B_{gₖ}` with the elementary-gate budget `CircuitBudget` — which folds
  a *product* of one-qubit gates one wire at a time (`CircuitBudget.listProd`) — `B` is exhibited
  as exactly such a product by
  `AxQM.Concrete.kronFamilyStd_pauliDiagGate_eq_prod_singleQubitOnWire`
  (`AxQM/Concrete/KronFamilyWireProduct.lean`):
  `B = ∏_{t<n} singleQubitOnWire finFunctionFinEquiv t (pauliDiagGate (g t))`, the ordered product
  of the `n` per-wire diagonalising gates. It is the Problem 4.3 instance of the flat
  factorisation `kronFamilyStd_eq_prod_singleQubitOnWire`
  (`⨂ₖ Aₖ = ∏_{t<n} singleQubitOnWire finFunctionFinEquiv t (Aₜ)`, from
  `kronFamily_eq_prod_kronWireGate` on the bit-string register and
  `singleQubitOnWire_eq_submatrix_kronWireGate`).
* **Part (3), the basis-change gate on a register wire is one single-qubit gate.** The per-wire
  factors of `B` above are single-qubit gates
  `singleQubitOnWire finFunctionFinEquiv t (pauliDiagGate (g t))` on the flat `2ⁿ`-level register;
  the elementary-gate budget `CircuitBudget` instead counts gates on the qubit control tower. Each
  diagonalising factor is a named register `Evolution`, indexed by the Pauli
  `a : Fin 4` (as Problem 4.1's phase layer is built from `phaseWireEvolution` indexed by
  a real angle): `AxQM.pauliDiagWireEvolution n t a`
  (`AxQM/Postulates/API/PauliDiagWire.lean`) is `pauliDiagGate a` placed on register wire
  `t` (the `quditGate` promotion of `singleQubitOnWire … t (pauliDiagGate a)`), with one-qubit form
  `AxQM.pauliDiagGateEvolution a`. The register lift
  `AxQM.quditPowTower_congr_pauliDiagWireEvolution`
  (`AxQM/Core/PauliDiagWireTower.lean`) transports it onto the tower as the single-wire
  gate `Evolution.qtowerSingleWire n t.rev (pauliDiagGateEvolution a)`, whence
  `AxQM.pauliDiagWireEvolution_circuitBudget`:
  `CircuitBudget (pauliDiagWireEvolution (n+1) t a) 0 1` — the basis-change gate on any wire costs
  one single-qubit gate. It is the basis-change analogue of
  `quditPowTower_congr_qftHadamardEvolution` / `quditPowTower_congr_phaseWireEvolution`.
* **Part (3), the basis-change layer `B` as an `Evolution`, with its `n`-gate budget.** The
  per-wire gates are assembled into the whole layer
  `AxQM.pauliDiagLayer n g := ∏_{t<n} pauliDiagWireEvolution n t (g t)`
  (`AxQM/Postulates/API/PauliDiagWire.lean`), the `List.prod` over `List.finRange n` — the
  analogue of Problem 4.1's `phaseGradientLayer`. Two facts pin it down. **Faithfulness:**
  `AxQM.pauliDiagLayer_op` proves `(pauliDiagLayer n g).op = Matrix.toEuclideanCLM
  (kronFamilyStd (fun k => pauliDiagGate (g k)))`, i.e. this `n`-gate `Evolution` is *exactly* the
  tensor basis change `B = ⨂ₖ B_{gₖ}` of `Concrete/PauliStringDiagonalize`, via the operator
  product law `pauliDiagWireEvolution_listProd_op` (`Matrix.toEuclideanCLM` is a `⋆`-algebra
  isomorphism, so it carries the matrix factorisation
  `Concrete.kronFamilyStd_pauliDiagGate_eq_prod_singleQubitOnWire` to the operator composition) —
  not merely some product of `n` gates. **Gate count:** `AxQM.pauliDiagLayer_circuitBudget`
  (`AxQM/Core/PauliDiagWireTower.lean`) proves `CircuitBudget (pauliDiagLayer (n+1) g) 0
  (n+1)` — the layer costs `n+1` single-qubit gates and no `≤ 2`-control gates — by transporting it
  onto the control tower (`quditPowTower_congr_pauliDiagLayer`, the analogue of
  `quditPowTower_congr_phaseGradientLayer`) into the product of `n+1` single-wire gates each
  budgeted `0 1`, then folding with `CircuitBudget.listProd`.
* **Part (3), the Figure-4.19 parity `CNOT` cascade (matrix form).** After
  the basis change reduces `exp(s · g)` to `exp(s · d(g))` with `d(g) = pauliToZ ∘ g` (`Z` on the
  string's support `S`, `I` elsewhere), the diagonal exponential `exp(s · Z^{⊗S})` — a phase on the
  parity `⊕_{i∈S} xᵢ` — is implemented by Nielsen & Chuang's Figure 4.19: a fan of `CNOT`s computes
  the parity onto one collector wire `c ∈ S`, a single-qubit `Z`-rotation acts there, and the fan is
  uncomputed. At the matrix level this is
  `AxQM.Concrete.pauliString_exp_smul_conj_cnotFanInto`
  (`AxQM/Concrete/PauliStringParityCascade.lean`): for the `CNOT` fan
  `V = Concrete.cnotFanInto c ts` (controls `ts`, target `c`) and any Pauli string `a` that is `Z`
  exactly on `insert c ts`, `exp(s · P_a) = V† · exp(s · P_{Z_c}) · V` — reading right-to-left,
  compute the parity (`V`), rotate the collector (`exp(s · Z_c)`), uncompute (`V†`). It reuses the
  Gottesman `CNOT`-fan of Theorem 10.6: `Concrete.act_cnotFanInto_collapse` /
  `sign_cnotFanInto_collapse` prove `V` conjugates the all-`Z` string on `insert c ts` back to the
  single generator `zGenPauli c` with no sign (the `z_c += z_t` propagation of `CNOT_{t→c}`, Figure
  10.7, *is* the parity accumulation), and the lemma
  `Matrix.exp_smul_conj_of_conj_eq` (packaging `Matrix.exp_units_conj` with the `s ·` scalar) pushes
  it through the exponential (supporting lemmas `cnotFanInto_act_eq_zGenPauli`,
  `cnotFanInto_toMatrix_conj_pauliString_eq_zGenPauli`). The `O(n)` gate count is
  `Concrete.length_cnotFanInto_le` (`|ts| ≤ n` `CNOT`s). Instantiating `a = pauliToZ ∘ g` chains
  it with `pauliStringStd_exp_smul_conj_kronFamilyStd_pauliDiagGate` (the basis change above) to
  reduce `exp(s · g)` for an arbitrary Pauli string to a single single-qubit `Z`-rotation conjugated
  by an `O(n)`-gate circuit.
* **Part (3), the elementary two-control-gate budget.** The Figure-4.19 parity fan consists of
  `≤ n` `CNOT`s and one collector `Z`-rotation; counting them with the elementary-gate budget
  `CircuitBudget` (`Core/CircuitBudget`) needs a `≤ 2`-control-gate *leaf* (a placed/tower
  `CNOT`/`Toffoli` is one gate) and a *fold* (a product of them costs one per factor) — the
  `Toffoli`/`CNOT`/`NOT` counterpart of the single-qubit `qtowerSingleWire_circuitBudget` +
  `CircuitBudget.listProd` that the phase layer uses. These are in
  `AxQM/Core/McNotCircuitBudget.lean`:
  `AxQM.Evolution.placedMcNot_circuitBudget` (for `S.card ≤ 2`,
  `CircuitBudget (Evolution.placedMcNot S t ht) 1 0` — a placed multiply-controlled `NOT` on the
  flat register `qubit ^⊗ₛ m` is one `≤ 2`-control gate, the `gs = [(S, t)]` case of
  `CircuitBudget.block` via the truth table `Evolution.placedMcNot_evolvePure_qubitBasis`);
  `AxQM.mcNotTowerGate_circuitBudget` (the same on the qubit control tower
  `qtower n qubit`, the flat leaf transported along `qtowerQubitTensorPow n` by
  `CircuitBudget.congr`); and `AxQM.CircuitBudget.listProd_twoControl` (a `List.prod` of
  gates each budgeted `1 0` costs `l.length 0` — the `≤ 2`-control twin of
  `CircuitBudget.listProd`). The parity fan's budget folds these.
* **Part (3), the parity fan `V` and collector `Z`-rotation as `Evolution`s, with their `O(n)` and
  `1`-gate budgets.** The Figure-4.19 pieces are register `Evolution`s on `qudit (2ⁿ)` with
  `CircuitBudget`s, folding the leaves above. The fan is `AxQM.cnotFanIntoEvolution n c ts`
  (`AxQM/Postulates/API/CnotFanEvolution.lean`), the ordered product
  `∏_{t ∈ ts} cnotWireEvolution n t c` of per-control `CNOT`s `CNOT_{t→c}`
  (`AxQM.cnotWireEvolution`, a single register `CNOT` = `mcNotWireEvolution n {a} b` for
  `a ≠ b`, identity otherwise) — the `Evolution` counterpart of the matrix fan
  `Concrete.cnotFanInto c ts`. Its cost is
  `AxQM.cnotFanIntoEvolution_circuitBudget`
  (`AxQM/Core/CnotFanEvolutionBudget.lean`):
  for a collector `c ∉ ts`, `CircuitBudget (cnotFanIntoEvolution (n+1) c ts) ts.length 0` — the
  fan's `ts.length = |S| − 1` `CNOT`s in `≤ 2`-control gates; with `V†` this is Figure 4.19's
  `2(|S| − 1) = O(n)` two-qubit gates. It transports the fan onto the tower
  (`Evolution.congr_prod`) and folds the per-`CNOT` leaves with
  `CircuitBudget.listProd_twoControl` (base `CircuitBudget.id_qtower`), each `CNOT` budgeted `1 0`
  by the register leaf `AxQM.mcNotWireEvolution_circuitBudget` (a register
  `Toffoli`/`CNOT`/`NOT` with `S.card ≤ 2` is one `≤ 2`-control gate, the register analogue of
  `mcNotTowerGate_circuitBudget`). The collector rotation is `rotZWireEvolution n c θ`
  with budget `AxQM.rotZWireEvolution_circuitBudget`
  (`CircuitBudget (rotZWireEvolution (n+1) t θ) 0 1` — one single-qubit gate for any angle, the
  `R_z` twin of `pauliDiagWireEvolution_circuitBudget`).
* **Part (3), the parity fan `V`: the register fan's operator is the Figure-4.19 matrix fan.** The
  fan `Evolution` carries the `O(n)` gate count; it is tied to the *matrix* fan
  `Concrete.cnotFanInto c ts`, whose parity collapse is `exp(s·P_a) = V†·exp(s·Z_c)·V`, by
  `AxQM.cnotFanIntoEvolution_op`
  (`AxQM/Postulates/API/CnotFanEvolution.lean`): the operator of the register fan is
  *exactly* that matrix fan, `(cnotFanIntoEvolution n c ts).op = Matrix.toEuclideanCLM
  ((cnotFanInto c ts).toMatrix.submatrix finFunctionFinEquiv.symm finFunctionFinEquiv.symm)` — the
  bit-string matrix reindexed to the flat register `Fin (2ⁿ)` along `finFunctionFinEquiv`, exactly
  the `.submatrix` that `pauliStringStd` / `kronFamilyStd` use (the literal `toEuclideanCLM
  (cnotFanInto …).toMatrix` would be an operator on the *bit-string* space `Fin n → Fin 2`, not the
  register). It rests on the single-`CNOT` statement `AxQM.cnotWireEvolution_op` (each
  `cnotWireEvolution n a b` is `toEuclideanCLM` of the reindexed step matrix
  `(cnotStep a b).toMatrix`)
  and folds along the fan's `List.prod` (`cnotFanIntoEvolution_cons` vs `cnotFanInto_cons`), with
  `Matrix.toEuclideanCLM`'s `map_mul` and `Matrix.submatrix_mul_equiv` distributing the promotion
  and reindexing over the product. The single-gate core is the matrix bridge
  `AxQM.Concrete.permGate_mcNotPerm_singleton_eq_submatrix_cnotWireGate`
  (`AxQM/Concrete/CnotWirePermGate.lean`): the one-control multiply-controlled-`NOT`
  permutation gate `permGate enc (mcNotPerm {a} …)` is the Clifford `cnotWireGate a b` reindexed
  along `enc` (both realise the same bit-permutation, `mcNot {a} b = cnotPerm a b`).
* **Part (3), the collector `Z`-rotation: `rotZWireEvolution`↔`exp(s·Z_c)`.** The collector
  rotation `Evolution` `rotZWireEvolution n c θ` carries the one-single-qubit-gate budget
  `rotZWireEvolution_circuitBudget`; it is tied to the *matrix* collector rotation
  `exp(s·P_{Z_c})` = `exp(s • pauliString (zGenPauli c))` that the parity collapse
  `exp(s·P_a) = V†·exp(s·Z_c)·V` rotates, by
  `AxQM.rotZWireEvolution_op_eq_exp_smul_zGenPauli`
  (`AxQM/Postulates/API/CollectorZRotationEvolution.lean`): the operator of the register
  `z`-rotation is *exactly* that collector exponential (`e := finFunctionFinEquiv`),
  `(rotZWireEvolution n c θ).op = Matrix.toEuclideanCLM ((exp((−θ/2)·i • pauliString`
  `(zGenPauli c))).submatrix e⁻¹ e⁻¹)` — the same `.submatrix finFunctionFinEquiv.symm` reindex to
  the flat register `Fin (2ⁿ)` that `cnotFanIntoEvolution_op` uses, with `s = (−θ/2)·i`
  (so the physical `θ = 2 h_g Δ` gives `s = -i h_g Δ`). Its matrix core is
  `AxQM.Concrete.exp_smul_pauliString_zGenPauli_submatrix_eq_singleQubitOnWire`
  (`AxQM/Concrete/CollectorZRotation.lean`): reindexed, the collector exponential is the
  `2×2` gate `exp(s • Z)` placed on wire `c` (`singleQubitOnWire e c (exp(s • Z))`), proved as a
  diagonal-vector comparison — `pauliString (zGenPauli c)` and `Z` are both diagonal
  (`pauliString_zGenPauli_eq_diagonal`, the general-wire form of the wire-`0`
  `pauliString_zGenPauli_zero_eq_diagonal`; `pauliZ_eq_diagonal`), so `exp_smul_diagonal` (`exp` of
  a diagonal is the diagonal of `exp`s), `Matrix.submatrix_diagonal_equiv`, and
  `singleQubitOnWire_diagonal` reindex both sides to the same diagonal. `quditGate_op` gives the
  register `z`-rotation's operator and `rotZ_eq_exp_smul_pauliZ` (`R_z(θ) = exp((−θ/2)·i • Z)`)
  identifies the block. This and the fan statement `cnotFanIntoEvolution_op` compose under
  `Matrix.toEuclideanCLM`'s `map_mul` / `Matrix.submatrix_mul_equiv` to give the whole
  `V†·(Z-rot)·V` at the operator level.
* **Part (3), the assembled Figure-4.19 circuit: the cascade implements the Trotter summand.** The
  five pieces are assembled into one register `Evolution` that computes exactly `exp(-i h_g g Δ)`.
  `AxQM.pauliStringExpCircuit n g c ts θ`
  (`AxQM/Postulates/API/PauliStringExpCircuit.lean`) is the compute–rotate–uncompute
  cascade `B · V† · R_z(θ) · V · B†` (`pauliDiagLayer` `B`; the fan `cnotFanIntoEvolution` `V` with
  its `Evolution` adjoint `V†`; the collector rotation `rotZWireEvolution`; `B†` the layer adjoint),
  for a collector `c` and remaining support wires `ts` (so `g`'s support is `insert c ts`). Two
  theorems pin it down. **Closed form:** `AxQM.pauliStringExpCircuit_op` — for a `g`
  supported exactly on `insert c ts`, `(pauliStringExpCircuit n g c ts θ).op = Matrix.toEuclideanCLM
  (cos(θ/2)·1 − (sin(θ/2)·i)·pauliStringStd g)`, the single-axis rotation about the Pauli string
  `g`. **Faithfulness:** `AxQM.pauliStringExpCircuit_op_eq_propagator` — with
  `θ = 2 c t`, `(pauliStringExpCircuit n g c ts (2 c t)).op =
  ((pauliStringHamiltonian g c).propagator 1 0 t).op`, i.e. the circuit's operator *is* the Trotter
  summand `exp(-i c t · g)` — for `c = h_g`, `t = Δ`, the operator part (3) asks to realise. The
  proof keeps the exponential on bit strings: the basis-change reduction
  `Concrete.pauliString_exp_smul_conj_kronFamily_pauliDiagGate` (the bit-string companion of
  the `Std` form above) and the parity collapse `pauliString_exp_smul_conj_cnotFanInto`
  compose to `B·V†·exp(s·Z_c)·V·B† = exp(s·g)` (`s = (−θ/2)·i`); the circuit operator is
  `toEuclideanCLM` of that five-fold product reindexed to the register (the `_op`
  lemmas above + `Matrix.submatrix_mul_equiv` + `map_mul`, the two adjoints supplying the
  conjugate transposes via `Matrix.toEuclideanCLM_conjTranspose_eq_adjoint`); and `exp(s·g)`
  collapses to the rotation because `pauliString g` is an involution
  (`NormedSpace.exp_smul_mul_I_of_mul_self_eq_one`, under `open scoped Matrix.Norms.Operator` for
  the `NormedAlgebra` instance).
* **Part (3), the assembled circuit's `O(n)` gate count.** The cascade
  `B ∘ V† ∘ R_z ∘ V ∘ B†` computes the right operator; that it does so in `O(n)` one- and two-qubit
  gates is
  `AxQM.pauliStringExpCircuit_circuitBudget`
  (`AxQM/Core/PauliStringExpCircuitBudget.lean`): for a collector `c ∉ ts` on the
  `(n+1)`-qubit register, `CircuitBudget (pauliStringExpCircuit (n+1) g c ts θ) (2 * ts.length)
  (2 * n + 3)` — at most `2·ts.length` two-qubit (`≤ 2`-control) and `2n+3` single-qubit gates.
  With `g`'s support `insert c ts` (so `ts.length = |support| − 1 ≤ n`) both counts are linear in
  the qubit number, exactly Problem 4.3(3)'s `O(n)`. It folds the per-piece budgets
  `pauliDiagLayer_circuitBudget` (`B`, `B†`: `n+1` single-qubit gates each),
  `cnotFanIntoEvolution_circuitBudget` (`V`, `V†`: `ts.length` two-qubit gates each) and
  `rotZWireEvolution_circuitBudget` (`R_z`: one single-qubit gate) along the composition with
  `CircuitBudget.comp` (budgets add), reusing each forward budget for its uncomputation via
  `CircuitBudget.adjoint` (reversing a circuit preserves the gate count exactly), and relaxes the
  folded sums to the closed form with `CircuitBudget.mono`. Together with
  `pauliStringExpCircuit_op_eq_propagator` this completes Problem 4.3(3): the Figure-4.19 circuit
  implements `exp(-i h_g g Δ)` in `O(n)` elementary gates.
* **Part (6), the identity Pauli string's global phase is one gate.**
  Part (6) assembles the whole approximation `U ≈ [∏_g exp(-i h_g g Δ)]ᵏ`: the `k`-fold power (part
  (5)) of one Trotter step, itself the ordered product over *all* `4ⁿ` Pauli strings `g` of the
  part-(3) circuits `exp(-i h_g g Δ)`. The product ranges over **every** `g : Fin n → Fin 4`,
  including the **identity string** `g = I^{⊗n}`, whose summand
  `exp(-i h_I · I · Δ) = e^{-i h_I Δ}·1` is a *global phase* — not a Pauli rotation, so the
  part-(3) circuit `pauliStringExpCircuit` (which needs a nonempty support / collector wire) does
  not cover it. Its gate count is `AxQM.globalPhaseGate_circuitBudget`
  (`AxQM/Core/GlobalPhaseCircuitBudget.lean`), which proves
  `CircuitBudget (globalPhaseGate α : Evolution (qudit (2ⁿ⁺¹))) 0 1` — a register global phase
  `e^{iα}·1` is one single-qubit gate, realised as the scalar gate `e^{iα}·1₂` on one wire
  (`(e^{iα}·1₂) ⊗ 1 = e^{iα}·1`). The proof is the global-phase analogue of
  `rotZWireEvolution_circuitBudget`: transport onto the control tower via `quditPowTower n` (the
  phase is transport-invariant, `Evolution.congr_globalPhaseGate`), identify it with the head-wire
  placement (`qtowerSingleWire_zero_globalPhaseGate`), and count it with
  `qtowerSingleWire_circuitBudget`.
* **Part (6), the total per-string Trotter summand as one register `Evolution`, faithful and
  `O(n)`.** The full Trotter step is the ordered product over *all* `4ⁿ`
  Pauli strings `g` of the summands `exp(-i h_g g Δ)`; for that product to be an `Evolution`-valued
  function of `g` each summand — including the identity string's global phase — must be a *single*
  register `Evolution`. `AxQM.pauliTrotterSummand n g coeff t`
  (`AxQM/Postulates/API/PauliTrotterSummand.lean`) is `exp(-i · coeff · t · g)` as one
  `Evolution (qudit (2ⁿ))`, by cases on the support `Concrete.pauliStringSupport g`: a nonempty
  support gives the Figure-4.19 cascade `pauliStringExpCircuit n g c ts (2 coeff t)` (with
  canonical collector `c = support.min'` and remaining support wires
  `ts = (support.erase c).toList`, so the support is exactly `insert c ts`), and the empty support
  (the identity string, whose summand is not a Pauli rotation) gives
  `globalPhaseGate (-(coeff t))`. **Faithfulness:** `AxQM.pauliTrotterSummand_op` proves
  `(pauliTrotterSummand n g coeff t).op = ((pauliStringHamiltonian g coeff).propagator 1 0 t).op`
  for *every* `g` — the nonempty case is
  `pauliStringExpCircuit_op_eq_propagator` (its five support hypotheses discharged from the
  canonical `c`/`ts`), the identity case matches
  `globalPhaseGate_op` against the propagator closed form `pauliStringHamiltonian_propagator_op` at
  `g = 0` (`(pauliStringHamiltonian g 1).op = 1` via `Concrete.pauliStringStd_zero`; Euler
  `Complex.exp_ofReal_mul_I`). **Gate count:** `AxQM.pauliTrotterSummand_circuitBudget`
  (`AxQM/Core/PauliTrotterSummandBudget.lean`) proves
  `CircuitBudget (pauliTrotterSummand (n+1) g coeff t) (2·n) (2·n+3)` — `O(n)` uniformly, relaxing
  the nonempty cascade's `pauliStringExpCircuit_circuitBudget` (`2·ts.length ≤ 2n` as `ts` is the
  support minus one wire) and the identity's `globalPhaseGate_circuitBudget` (`0 1`) to the common
  bound with `CircuitBudget.mono`.
* **Part (6), one full Trotter step as one register `Evolution`, faithful and `O(n·4ⁿ)`.**
  The `k`-fold approximation `[∏_g exp(-i h_g g Δ)]ᵏ` is the `k`-th power of a
  single **Trotter step** — the ordered product over *all* `4ⁿ` Pauli strings `g` of the
  summands `pauliTrotterSummand`.
  `AxQM.pauliTrotterStep n h t`
  (`AxQM/Postulates/API/PauliTrotterStep.lean`) is
  `∏_g exp(-i · h_g · t · g)` as one `Evolution (qudit (2ⁿ))`, the `List.prod` over the fixed list
  `Finset.univ.toList` of all `4ⁿ` strings of `pauliTrotterSummand n g (h g) t` — the same ordering
  as N&C's part-(4)/(5) forms in `Postulates/API/PauliTrotter`. **Faithfulness:**
  `AxQM.pauliTrotterStep_op` proves `(pauliTrotterStep n h t).op =
  (Finset.univ.toList.map fun g => ((pauliStringHamiltonian g (h g)).propagator 1 0 t).op).prod` —
  *exactly* the part-(5) propagator product — by pushing the uniform summand faithfulness
  `pauliTrotterSummand_op` through `Evolution.op_prod` (the operator of an `Evolution`-monoid
  product is the product of the operators). This carries N&C's operator-level
  parts (4)/(5) onto the circuit: at `t = 1/k`, `(pauliTrotterStep n h (1/k)).op ^ k` is the base
  (to the `k`) of the theorem `Evolution.exists_pauliString_trotter_approximation`. **Gate count:**
  `AxQM.pauliTrotterStep_circuitBudget`
  (`AxQM/Core/PauliTrotterStepBudget.lean`) proves
  `CircuitBudget (pauliTrotterStep (n+1) h t) (4ⁿ⁺¹ · 2n) (4ⁿ⁺¹ · (2n+3))` — `O(n·4ⁿ)`, by folding
  the uniform per-summand budget `pauliTrotterSummand_circuitBudget (2n, 2n+3)` over the product
  with `CircuitBudget.listProd_uniform` (number of factors
  `= Fintype.card (Fin (n+1) → Fin 4) = 4ⁿ⁺¹`), the empty-product base being the register
  identity budget `CircuitBudget.id_qudit` (the qudit-register analogue of
  `CircuitBudget.id_qtower`, beside it in `Core/PhaseLayerBudget`). The Trotter step is raised to
  the `k`-th power with `Evolution.op_pow`, the part-(5) `O(1/k)` bound is transferred onto it via
  `pauliTrotterStep_op`, and the `O(n·4ⁿ)` budget is scaled by `k` (`CircuitBudget` of a power).
* **Part (6), the Pauli-coefficient `ℓ¹` bound (matrix form).** N&C's literal `O(n·16ⁿ/ε)` needs a
  Trotter constant `C = O(4ⁿ)`, which is `Λ² = O(4ⁿ)` for the total Pauli weight
  `Λ = ∑_g |h_g|` of `H = i ln U` (so `Λ = O(2ⁿ)`). The **matrix core** of that estimate is in
  `AxQM/Concrete/PauliCoefficientBound.lean`. For a Hermitian `M` with real Pauli expansion
  `M = ∑_g (h_g : ℂ) • P_g` (part (2), `Concrete.exists_real_pauliString_expansion`):
  `AxQM.Concrete.trace_conjTranspose_mul_self_re_eq_sum_pauliCoeff_sq` is the **Parseval
  identity** `(Mᴴ * M).trace.re = 2ⁿ · ∑_g h_g²` (Hilbert–Schmidt orthogonality of the Pauli strings
  `pauliString_trace_mul`: `tr(P_g P_{g'}) = 2ⁿ δ_{g g'}` collapses `tr(M²)` to `2ⁿ ∑ h_g²`), and
  `AxQM.Concrete.sq_sum_abs_pauliCoeff_le` is the **`ℓ¹` bound**
  `(∑_g |h_g|)² ≤ 2ⁿ · (Mᴴ * M).trace.re` — Cauchy–Schwarz over the `4ⁿ` coefficients
  (`sq_sum_le_card_mul_sum_sq`, `(∑|h_g|)² ≤ 4ⁿ ∑ h_g²`) then Parseval turns the `4ⁿ` into `2ⁿ`.
* **Part (6), the total Pauli weight `Λ = ∑_g |h_g| = O(2ⁿ)`.** The matrix core above is promoted
  to observables and closed off into the full `O(2ⁿ)` coefficient estimate in
  `AxQM/Postulates/API/PauliCoefficientOpNorm.lean`. Four declarations.
  `AxQM.Observable.trace_stdMatrix_conjTranspose_mul_self_re_le` is the **Hilbert–Schmidt
  vs. operator-norm** bridge `(H.stdMatrixᴴ * H.stdMatrix).trace.re ≤ 2ⁿ · ‖H.op‖²`: the `(a,a)`
  diagonal entry of `Aᴴ A` (`A = H.stdMatrix`) is the column-`a` squared norm
  `∑_i ‖⟪|i⟩, H.op|a⟩⟫‖² = ‖H.op|a⟩‖²` — a **Parseval** sum over the computational orthonormal basis
  (`OrthonormalBasis.sum_sq_norm_inner_right`) — and `‖H.op|a⟩‖ ≤ ‖H.op‖` as `|a⟩` is a unit vector
  (`PureState.normalized`), so the `2ⁿ` diagonal entries sum to `≤ 2ⁿ ‖H.op‖²` (the operator-norm
  form of `tr(MᴴM) = ∑σ_i² ≤ d‖M‖²`). `AxQM.Observable.sum_abs_pauliCoeff_le` is the
  op-norm coefficient bound `∑_g |h_g| ≤ 2ⁿ · ‖H.op‖` for **any** real Pauli expansion: chaining
  `Concrete.sq_sum_abs_pauliCoeff_le` (`(∑|h_g|)² ≤ 2ⁿ (Mᴴ M).trace.re`, `M` the
  bit-string-reindexed `H.stdMatrix`) with the trace bridge (the trace is reindex-invariant) gives
  `(∑|h_g|)² ≤ 2ⁿ·2ⁿ‖H.op‖² = (2ⁿ‖H.op‖)²`, and both sides are nonnegative. And
  `AxQM.Observable.sum_abs_pauliCoeff_le_of_spectrum_Icc` layers the part-(1b) spectrum on
  top: for a Hamiltonian with `spectrum ℂ H.op ⊆ [0, c]`, `∑_g |h_g| ≤ 2ⁿ · c`, via the
  self-adjoint bound `AxQM.Observable.opNorm_le_of_spectrum_subset_Icc` (`‖H.op‖ ≤ c` — for
  a self-adjoint operator the norm is the spectral radius `⨆ ‖k‖`, and each eigenvalue `k = r ≤ c`,
  `ContinuousLinearMap.spectralRadius_eq_nnNorm`). With `c = 2π` (the generator of a unitary,
  `Evolution.exists_hamiltonian_generator_spectrum_Icc`) this is exactly `Λ = ∑_g |h_g| ≤ 2ⁿ·2π =
  O(2ⁿ)`, so `Λ² = O(4ⁿ)`.
* **Part (6), the literal `O(n·16ⁿ/ε)` gate count.** The statement
  `Evolution.exists_pauliTrotterStep_pow_op_sub_le_explicit`
  (`Postulates/API/PauliTrotterExplicit.lean`) sharpens the part-(5) circuit accuracy to the
  **explicit** `‖(pauliTrotterStep (n+1) h (1/k))ᵏ.op - U.op‖ ≤ trotterProductConst·(2ⁿ⁺¹·2π)²·k⁻¹`
  — an `O(4ⁿ⁺¹)/k` bound with a fully numerical constant (the unitarity-aware first-order Trotter
  bound's `Λ²` weight times `Λ ≤ 2ⁿ⁺¹·2π`). It is used below to complete Part (6). Two theorems
  close the item. `Evolution.exists_pauliString_trotter_circuit_approximation_explicit`
  restates the scaffold `Evolution.exists_pauliString_trotter_circuit_approximation`
  with the **explicit** step-count bound `k ≤ trotterProductConst·(2ⁿ⁺¹·2π)²/ε + 1` in place of the
  general Banach `C` — `k = O(4ⁿ⁺¹/ε)`, N&C's factor (ii). And
  `Evolution.exists_pauliString_trotter_circuit_gateCount` multiplies factor (ii) by the per-step
  `O(n·4ⁿ)` `CircuitBudget` (factor (i)) to give the **literal** total gate count
  `k·4ⁿ⁺¹·(4n+3) ≤ trotterProductConst·4π²·(4n+3)·16ⁿ⁺¹/ε + 4ⁿ⁺¹·(4n+3)` — exactly N&C's
  `O(n·16ⁿ/ε)` for the `n+1`-qubit register (`16ⁿ⁺¹ = 4ⁿ⁺¹·4ⁿ⁺¹`, absolute constant
  `trotterProductConst·4π²`), with the `ε`-independent additive `4ⁿ⁺¹·(4n+3) = Θ(n·4ⁿ)` the rounding
  term of `k = ⌈C/ε⌉`.
-/

open scoped InnerProductSpace

noncomputable section

namespace AxQM

/-- **Nielsen & Chuang, Problem 4.3 (1): the Hamiltonian generator exists.** Every closed-system
`Evolution U` on a finite-dimensional quantum system `S` is generated by a Hamiltonian: there is an
`Observable H` (a self-adjoint operator) with `U = H.propagator 1 0 1`, i.e. `U = exp(-iH)`. This is
the content of "`H = i ln(U)` is Hermitian" — the unitary `U` is the time-`1` propagator of some
Hermitian `H` — and is the finite-dimensional converse of `Observable.propagator` (N&C Exercise
2.55, "`exp(-iH)` is unitary").

The eigenvalue-range refinement of Part (1) (`H` chosen with spectrum in `[0, 2π]`) is a separate,
stronger statement about the branch of `ln`; see the module docstring. -/
theorem Evolution.exists_hamiltonian_generator {S : QSystem} (U : Evolution S) :
    ∃ H : Observable S, U = H.propagator 1 0 1 := by
  have : CompleteSpace S.space := FiniteDimensional.completeSpace ℂ S.space
  -- The unitary `U.op` is `exp(i • A)` for a self-adjoint `A` (operator form of N&C Ex. 2.56).
  obtain ⟨A, hAsa, hAexp⟩ :=
    ContinuousLinearMap.exists_isSelfAdjoint_exp_smul_I_eq_of_mem_unitary U.unitary
  -- Take the Hamiltonian `H = -A`; then `exp(-i H) = exp(i • A) = U.op`.
  refine ⟨⟨-A, hAsa.neg⟩, Evolution.ext ?_⟩
  rw [Observable.propagator_op, ← hAexp]
  congr 1
  -- `(-i · (1-0)/1) • (-A) = i • A`
  rw [show (-Complex.I * ((1 : ℝ) - 0 : ℝ) / ((1 : ℝ) : ℂ)) = -Complex.I by push_cast; ring,
    smul_neg, neg_smul, neg_neg]

/-- **The extraction step shared by the two circuit-approximation statements below.** From a
`C/k`-decay bound on the `k`-fold Pauli–Trotter circuit's gate error — for every `k ≥ 1`,
`(pauliTrotterStep (n+1) h (1/k))ᵏ` lies within `C · k⁻¹` of `U` — together with the per-step gate
budget `pauliTrotterStep_pow_circuitBudget`, extract the `ε`-approximation scaffold: for every
accuracy `ε > 0` a repetition count `k ≥ 1` with `gateError ≤ ε`, the explicit `CircuitBudget`, and
`k ≤ C/ε + 1` (take `k = ⌈C/ε⌉`, at least `1`; the `C/k` decay then drops below `ε`).

This is the common core of both `Evolution.exists_pauliString_trotter_circuit_approximation` (the
general Banach `C`) and `Evolution.exists_pauliString_trotter_circuit_approximation_explicit` (the
explicit `C = trotterProductConst · (2ⁿ⁺¹·2π)²`): the two differ only in *which* `C/k` operator
bound they feed in. The hypothesis is phrased with `Evolution.gateError` rather than
`‖·.op - ·.op‖` (the two are definitionally equal, so an operator-norm decay bound discharges it
directly). -/
theorem exists_pauliTrotterStep_pow_circuit_approximation_of_gateError_le {n : ℕ}
    {U : Evolution (qudit (2 ^ (n + 1)))} {h : (Fin (n + 1) → Fin 4) → ℝ} {C : ℝ} (hC0 : 0 ≤ C)
    (hbd : ∀ k : ℕ, 1 ≤ k →
      ((pauliTrotterStep (n + 1) h (k : ℝ)⁻¹) ^ k).gateError U ≤ C * (k : ℝ)⁻¹) :
    ∀ ε : ℝ, 0 < ε → ∃ k : ℕ, 1 ≤ k ∧
      ((pauliTrotterStep (n + 1) h (k : ℝ)⁻¹) ^ k).gateError U ≤ ε ∧
      CircuitBudget ((pauliTrotterStep (n + 1) h (k : ℝ)⁻¹) ^ k)
        (k * (4 ^ (n + 1) * (2 * n))) (k * (4 ^ (n + 1) * (2 * n + 3))) ∧
      (k : ℝ) ≤ C / ε + 1 := by
  intro ε hε
  -- Take `k = ⌈C/ε⌉` (at least `1`); the `C/k` decay drops below `ε`.
  refine ⟨max 1 ⌈C / ε⌉₊, le_max_left _ _, ?_, pauliTrotterStep_pow_circuitBudget h _ _, ?_⟩
  · -- accuracy: `gateError ≤ C/k ≤ ε`
    set k : ℕ := max 1 ⌈C / ε⌉₊
    have hk1 : 1 ≤ k := le_max_left _ _
    have hkR : (0 : ℝ) < k := by exact_mod_cast Nat.lt_of_lt_of_le Nat.zero_lt_one hk1
    have hceil : C / ε ≤ (k : ℝ) :=
      le_trans (Nat.le_ceil (C / ε)) (by exact_mod_cast le_max_right 1 ⌈C / ε⌉₊)
    refine le_trans (hbd k hk1) ?_
    rw [mul_inv_le_iff₀ hkR]
    rw [div_le_iff₀ hε] at hceil
    linarith
  · -- repetition count: `k ≤ C/ε + 1`
    have hCε : (0 : ℝ) ≤ C / ε := div_nonneg hC0 (le_of_lt hε)
    rw [Nat.cast_max]
    exact max_le (by linarith) (le_of_lt (Nat.ceil_lt_add_one hCε))

/-- **Nielsen & Chuang, Problem 4.3(6): circuit approximation with a general Trotter
constant.** Every
unitary `U` on `n+1` qubits is approximated to within any accuracy `ε > 0` by an explicit circuit —
the `k`-fold power of one Pauli–Trotter step `∏_g exp(-i h_g g /k)` — whose gate count is
`k · O(n·4ⁿ)`, with the number of repetitions `k` bounded by `C/ε + 1` for the library's *current*
first-order Trotter constant `C` (parts (4)/(5)).

Precisely: for every `U : Evolution (qudit (2ⁿ⁺¹))` there are real Pauli-string coefficients
`h : (Fin (n+1) → Fin 4) → ℝ` (the coefficients of `H = i ln U = Σ_g h_g g`, part (2)) and a
constant `C ≥ 0` (the first-order Trotter constant, parts (4)/(5)) such that for every `ε > 0` there
is a Trotter repetition count `k ≥ 1` for which the circuit `(pauliTrotterStep (n+1) h (1/k))ᵏ`

* **approximates `U` within `ε`** — `gateError ≤ ε`, i.e. `‖(circuit).op - U.op‖ ≤ ε`
  (N&C eq. 4.61);
* **costs `k · 4ⁿ⁺¹ · 2n` two-qubit and `k · 4ⁿ⁺¹ · (2n+3)` single-qubit gates** — the explicit
  `CircuitBudget`; and
* **needs only `k ≤ C/ε + 1` repetitions**.

It assembles part (1) (`U = exp(-iH)` for a Hermitian `H`), part (2) (`H = Σ_g h_g g`), part (5)
(the `O(1/k)` accuracy `Evolution.exists_pauliTrotterStep_pow_op_sub_le`), and part (3) (the
per-step `O(n·4ⁿ)` gate count `pauliTrotterStep_pow_circuitBudget`), choosing `k = ⌈C/ε⌉` to drive
the accuracy below `ε`.

**N&C's `O(n·16ⁿ/ε)` is rigorous; this statement does not assert it.** N&C's `16ⁿ = 4ⁿ · 4ⁿ`
factors as (i) the `4ⁿ⁺¹` gates *per Trotter step* — explicit here in the `CircuitBudget` — times
(ii) the `O(4ⁿ)` *number of steps* `k`. Factor (ii) is the claim `k = O(4ⁿ/ε)`, i.e. `C = O(4ⁿ)`,
and it **is a rigorous bound**: for a first-order Trotter product of *unitaries*, the accumulated
error obeys the commutator-scaling bound `‖(∏_g e^{-i h_g g /k})ᵏ - e^{-iH}‖ ≤ O(Λ²)/k` with
`Λ = Σ_g |h_g|` and **no** exponential prefactor — the exponentials have operator norm `1`, so the
noncommutative telescoping `norm_pow_sub_pow_le` runs with `M = 1` (unlike the crude
`M = exp(k⁻¹Σ‖gᵢ‖)` of the general Banach lemma
`NormedSpace.exists_norm_prod_exp_inv_smul_pow_sub_exp_sum_le`, which the current `C` comes from and
which never uses that the `gᵢ` are anti-Hermitian). Part (1) gives `‖H‖ ≤ 2π`, whence
`Λ = Σ_g |h_g| = O(2ⁿ)` (Cauchy–Schwarz over the `4ⁿ` coefficients, `Σ_g |h_g|² = ‖H‖²_F/2ⁿ ≤
(2π)²`), so `Λ² = O(4ⁿ)`, `k = O(4ⁿ/ε)`, and the total is exactly N&C's `O(n·16ⁿ/ε)`.

This theorem exhibits the circuit and its *exact* per-step `4ⁿ⁺¹`-gate `CircuitBudget`, but bounds
`k` by `C/ε + 1` for the general first-order Trotter constant `C` (the general Banach constant,
which carries the crude `exp(Λ)` factor and is therefore not pinned to `O(4ⁿ)`). Reaching N&C's
literal `16ⁿ` needs two ingredients: **(α)** the unitarity-aware first-order Trotter bound with an
explicit `O(Λ²)` constant (a Banach/`C⋆`-algebra argument, telescoping with `M = 1`), and **(β)**
the coefficient estimate `Σ_g |h_g| = O(2ⁿ)` from the part-(1) spectrum bound. It does not assert
`16ⁿ` at all.

The sharper statements with explicit constant are
`Evolution.exists_pauliString_trotter_circuit_approximation_explicit` (the same statement, but with
the concrete `C = trotterProductConst · (2ⁿ⁺¹·2π)² = O(4ⁿ⁺¹)` in place of the general Banach
constant) and `Evolution.exists_pauliString_trotter_circuit_gateCount` (the literal
`O(n·16ⁿ/ε)` total gate count), both below.

The accuracy is proved from `Evolution.exists_pauliTrotterStep_pow_op_sub_le` (`gateError` unfolds
to the operator-norm bound), the gate count from `pauliTrotterStep_pow_circuitBudget`. -/
theorem Evolution.exists_pauliString_trotter_circuit_approximation {n : ℕ}
    (U : Evolution (qudit (2 ^ (n + 1)))) :
    ∃ (h : (Fin (n + 1) → Fin 4) → ℝ) (C : ℝ), 0 ≤ C ∧ ∀ ε : ℝ, 0 < ε → ∃ k : ℕ, 1 ≤ k ∧
      ((pauliTrotterStep (n + 1) h (k : ℝ)⁻¹) ^ k).gateError U ≤ ε ∧
      CircuitBudget ((pauliTrotterStep (n + 1) h (k : ℝ)⁻¹) ^ k)
        (k * (4 ^ (n + 1) * (2 * n))) (k * (4 ^ (n + 1) * (2 * n + 3))) ∧
      (k : ℝ) ≤ C / ε + 1 := by
  obtain ⟨h, C, hC0, hbd⟩ := U.exists_pauliTrotterStep_pow_op_sub_le
  exact ⟨h, C, hC0, exists_pauliTrotterStep_pow_circuit_approximation_of_gateError_le hC0 hbd⟩

/-- **Nielsen & Chuang, Problem 4.3(6): the circuit approximation with an explicit
`O(4ⁿ)` constant.** The sharpening of `Evolution.exists_pauliString_trotter_circuit_approximation`
in which the abstract first-order Trotter constant `C` is replaced by the **fully explicit**
`trotterProductConst · (2ⁿ⁺¹ · 2π)²`. Every unitary `U` on `n+1` qubits is approximated to within
any `ε > 0` by the `k`-fold Pauli–Trotter circuit `(pauliTrotterStep (n+1) h (1/k))ᵏ`, and the
number of
repetitions is bounded by `k ≤ trotterProductConst · (2ⁿ⁺¹ · 2π)² / ε + 1`.

Since `(2ⁿ⁺¹ · 2π)² = 4ⁿ⁺¹ · (2π)²`, the step-count constant is **manifestly `O(4ⁿ⁺¹)`** — no
longer existential and no longer carrying the crude `exp(∑|h_g|)` prefactor of the general Banach
bound. This is N&C's `k = O(4ⁿ/ε)` (factor (ii) of `16ⁿ = 4ⁿ · 4ⁿ`); combined with the per-step
`O(n·4ⁿ)` `CircuitBudget` (factor (i)) it gives the literal `O(n·16ⁿ/ε)` total — see
`Evolution.exists_pauliString_trotter_circuit_gateCount` below.

**Proof.** Applies the shared extraction step
`exists_pauliTrotterStep_pow_circuit_approximation_of_gateError_le` (the `k = ⌈C/ε⌉` choice, the
`C/k`-below-`ε` accuracy step, and the per-step budget `pauliTrotterStep_pow_circuitBudget`) to the
**explicit** operator bound `Evolution.exists_pauliTrotterStep_pow_op_sub_le_explicit`
(`Postulates/API/PauliTrotterExplicit.lean`, the α + β result combining α and β) — the very same
step that `Evolution.exists_pauliString_trotter_circuit_approximation` applies to the
existential `Evolution.exists_pauliTrotterStep_pow_op_sub_le`. The two scaffolds differ only in
which `C/k` bound they feed. -/
theorem Evolution.exists_pauliString_trotter_circuit_approximation_explicit {n : ℕ}
    (U : Evolution (qudit (2 ^ (n + 1)))) :
    ∃ (h : (Fin (n + 1) → Fin 4) → ℝ), ∀ ε : ℝ, 0 < ε → ∃ k : ℕ, 1 ≤ k ∧
      ((pauliTrotterStep (n + 1) h (k : ℝ)⁻¹) ^ k).gateError U ≤ ε ∧
      CircuitBudget ((pauliTrotterStep (n + 1) h (k : ℝ)⁻¹) ^ k)
        (k * (4 ^ (n + 1) * (2 * n))) (k * (4 ^ (n + 1) * (2 * n + 3))) ∧
      (k : ℝ) ≤ NormedSpace.trotterProductConst * (2 ^ (n + 1) * (2 * Real.pi)) ^ 2 / ε + 1 := by
  obtain ⟨h, hbd⟩ := U.exists_pauliTrotterStep_pow_op_sub_le_explicit
  have hC0 : 0 ≤ NormedSpace.trotterProductConst * (2 ^ (n + 1) * (2 * Real.pi)) ^ 2 :=
    mul_nonneg NormedSpace.trotterProductConst_nonneg (sq_nonneg _)
  exact ⟨h, exists_pauliTrotterStep_pow_circuit_approximation_of_gateError_le hC0 hbd⟩

/-- **Nielsen & Chuang, Problem 4.3(6): the literal `O(n·16ⁿ/ε)` gate count.** The headline of
Part (6): *every* unitary `U` on `n+1` qubits can be approximated to within any accuracy `ε > 0` by
an explicit one- and two-qubit circuit — the `k`-fold Pauli–Trotter step
`(pauliTrotterStep (n+1) h (1/k))ᵏ` — whose **total gate count is `O(n·16ⁿ/ε)`**:
`total ≤ trotterProductConst · 4π² · (4n+3) · 16ⁿ⁺¹ / ε + 4ⁿ⁺¹ · (4n+3)`.

This is N&C's `16ⁿ` made literal. It factorises exactly as N&C's argument does, `16ⁿ⁺¹ = 4ⁿ⁺¹·4ⁿ⁺¹`:

* factor (i), the `4ⁿ⁺¹` gates **per Trotter step** — the explicit `CircuitBudget`
  `k · 4ⁿ⁺¹ · (2n)` two-qubit and `k · 4ⁿ⁺¹ · (2n+3)` single-qubit gates (parts (3)/(4)/(5)), whose
  per-step total is `4ⁿ⁺¹ · (4n+3)`;
* factor (ii), the `O(4ⁿ⁺¹/ε)` **number of steps** `k` — the explicit step-count bound
  `k ≤ trotterProductConst · (2ⁿ⁺¹·2π)² / ε + 1` from
  `Evolution.exists_pauliString_trotter_circuit_approximation_explicit`, i.e. `k = O(4ⁿ⁺¹/ε)`
  because `(2ⁿ⁺¹·2π)² = 4ⁿ⁺¹·4π²`.

Multiplying, the total `k · 4ⁿ⁺¹ · (4n+3)` is `≤ (trotterProductConst·4π²·16ⁿ⁺¹/ε + 4ⁿ⁺¹)·(4n+3)`,
with the leading `ε`-dependent coefficient `trotterProductConst · 4π² · (4n+3) · 16ⁿ⁺¹` — genuinely
`Θ(n·16ⁿ)` — and an `ε`-independent additive `4ⁿ⁺¹·(4n+3) = Θ(n·4ⁿ)` (the unavoidable `+1` from
rounding the step count `k = ⌈C/ε⌉` up to an integer, lower-order as `ε → 0`). This is exactly
N&C's `O(n·16ⁿ/ε)` for the register of `n+1` qubits (N&C's "`n` qubits" is our `n+1`). It is not a
weakened claim: the `16ⁿ⁺¹` and the absolute constant `trotterProductConst·4π²` are exhibited, not
hidden behind an `O(·)`.

**Proof.** For each `ε` take the circuit, accuracy, budget and explicit step count `k ≤ C/ε + 1`
(`C = trotterProductConst·(2ⁿ⁺¹·2π)²`) of the explicit scaffold; the total gate count
`k · 4ⁿ⁺¹ · (4n+3)` (the sum of the two `CircuitBudget` entries) is monotone in `k`, so plugging in
`k ≤ C/ε + 1` and rewriting `C · 4ⁿ⁺¹ = trotterProductConst · 4π² · 16ⁿ⁺¹` (base algebra
`(2ⁿ⁺¹)² = 4ⁿ⁺¹`, `(4ⁿ⁺¹)² = 16ⁿ⁺¹`) gives the stated bound. -/
theorem Evolution.exists_pauliString_trotter_circuit_gateCount {n : ℕ}
    (U : Evolution (qudit (2 ^ (n + 1)))) :
    ∃ (h : (Fin (n + 1) → Fin 4) → ℝ), ∀ ε : ℝ, 0 < ε → ∃ k : ℕ, 1 ≤ k ∧
      ((pauliTrotterStep (n + 1) h (k : ℝ)⁻¹) ^ k).gateError U ≤ ε ∧
      CircuitBudget ((pauliTrotterStep (n + 1) h (k : ℝ)⁻¹) ^ k)
        (k * (4 ^ (n + 1) * (2 * n))) (k * (4 ^ (n + 1) * (2 * n + 3))) ∧
      ((k * (4 ^ (n + 1) * (2 * n)) + k * (4 ^ (n + 1) * (2 * n + 3)) : ℕ) : ℝ)
        ≤ NormedSpace.trotterProductConst * (4 * Real.pi ^ 2) *
            ((4 * (n : ℝ) + 3) * 16 ^ (n + 1)) / ε
          + 4 ^ (n + 1) * (4 * (n : ℝ) + 3) := by
  obtain ⟨h, hbd⟩ := U.exists_pauliString_trotter_circuit_approximation_explicit
  refine ⟨h, fun ε hε => ?_⟩
  obtain ⟨k, hk1, hacc, hbudget, hkbound⟩ := hbd ε hε
  refine ⟨k, hk1, hacc, hbudget, ?_⟩
  -- total gate count `k · 4ⁿ⁺¹ · (4n+3)` is monotone in `k ≤ C/ε + 1`
  have hnn : (0 : ℝ) ≤ (4 : ℝ) ^ (n + 1) * (4 * (n : ℝ) + 3) := by positivity
  -- base conversions relating the powers of `2`, `4`, `16`
  have e1 : (4 : ℝ) ^ (n + 1) = (2 : ℝ) ^ (n + 1) * (2 : ℝ) ^ (n + 1) := by
    rw [← mul_pow]; norm_num
  have e2 : (16 : ℝ) ^ (n + 1) = (4 : ℝ) ^ (n + 1) * (4 : ℝ) ^ (n + 1) := by
    rw [← mul_pow]; norm_num
  calc ((k * (4 ^ (n + 1) * (2 * n)) + k * (4 ^ (n + 1) * (2 * n + 3)) : ℕ) : ℝ)
      = (k : ℝ) * ((4 : ℝ) ^ (n + 1) * (4 * (n : ℝ) + 3)) := by push_cast; ring
    _ ≤ (NormedSpace.trotterProductConst * (2 ^ (n + 1) * (2 * Real.pi)) ^ 2 / ε + 1)
          * ((4 : ℝ) ^ (n + 1) * (4 * (n : ℝ) + 3)) := mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_right hkbound hnn
    _ = NormedSpace.trotterProductConst * (4 * Real.pi ^ 2) *
            ((4 * (n : ℝ) + 3) * 16 ^ (n + 1)) / ε
          + 4 ^ (n + 1) * (4 * (n : ℝ) + 3) := by rw [e2, e1]; ring

end AxQM
